<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thutmose I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1506–1493 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thutmose I was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. He led military campaigns deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, greatly extending Egyptian power. Accounts of the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom treat his reign as part of the empire's foundational expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thutmose-i.jpg" alt="Thutmose I" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egyptian_Sandstone_Head_of_Thutmose_I,_Karnak_(Thebes),_18th_Dynasty_(35727582483).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thutmose I was the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of the 18th Dynasty and came to the throne after the death of Amenhotep I. He never called himself Son of the King, so he is believed to have been born outside the royal family, to a woman named Senseneb and an unknown father. His wife, Queen Ahmose, was probably a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a> and a sister of Amenhotep I, though she was never called king's daughter and some historians think she was Thutmose's sister. His children included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> and Nefrubity, by Ahmose, and a son by Mutnofret who became Thutmose II.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> rebelled when Thutmose was crowned. According to the tomb autobiography of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-son-of-ebana">Ahmose, son of Ebana</a>, the king sailed up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and personally killed the Nubian king, and hung his body from the prow of his ship. In his third year he ordered the canal at the first cataract, dug under Sesostris III, to be cleared so ships could pass upstream. A stela at Tombos records a fortress near the third cataract, and an inscription of his reign at Kurgus is the farthest south that Egyptian presence is attested. He appointed a viceroy of Kush, Turi.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> he campaigned as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, which he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s northern border. Texts of his time mention Retenu, Naharin and the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>, thought to be the first historical reference to that kingdom. As many as 20 sites in the Levant were destroyed in this period, though Egypt set up no permanent presence there. His largest building works were at the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, under the architect Ineni: a fourth and a fifth pylon, a hypostyle hall with cedar columns, and two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Thutmose I is the first king definitely buried in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a>, and Ineni dug his tomb. He was originally buried and then reburied in KV20, jointly with his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, and later moved by his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> to KV38, which was plundered late in the 20th Dynasty. He was succeeded by his son Thutmose II, and then by Hatshepsut. A mummy found in the Deir el-Bahri cache was long thought to be his, but in 2007 it was announced to be that of a man of about thirty who died of an arrow wound, probably not Thutmose I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thutmose I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors pressed aggressively outward: <b>Thutmose I</b> crossed the Euphrates, the first pharaoh to do so, and carried Egyptian arms beyond the fourth Nile cataract in Nubia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thutmose I</b> led several campaigns south that destroyed Kerma and ended in Egypt’s annexation of Nubia around 1504 BCE, with a frontier at Kanisah Kurgus beyond the Fourth Cataract.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thutmose I</b> erected an enclosure wall connecting the Fourth and Fifth pylons, which make up the earliest part of the temple still standing in situ.</blockquote><a href="/work/karnak">Karnak · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>…and decorated the royal tombs in the Valley of the Kings; their village dates from the time of <b>Thutmose I</b>. They lived with their families in a walled village of about 68 houses and worked in two gangs under foremen, eight days out of ten, for a monthly…</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thutmose I</b> sacked Kerma around 1500 BCE, and Egypt annexed Nubia as the Viceroyalty of Kush.</blockquote><a href="/state/kerma">Kerma</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Thutmose I</b> destroyed Kerma around 1500 BCE, the Egyptian Empire New Kingdom annexed Nubia as far as the Fourth Cataract.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-kush">Viceroyalty of Kush</a></li></ul></section>
